const themes = {
  'seasons': `    },\n    'second-grade': {\n      intro: 'Second graders bring a sophisticated understanding of cyclical patterns and a growing capacity for data analysis to seasons worksheets, ready to explore seasonal change through the lens of real measurement, comparative research, and multi-step mathematical reasoning. Seven- and eight-year-olds can add and subtract within 100, interpret simple bar graphs and pictographs, read multi-paragraph informational texts with comprehension, and write organized paragraphs with supporting details. Seasons-themed math worksheets at this level present problems such as the average high temperature in March was 52 degrees and in June it was 78 degrees, how much warmer was June than March, challenging children to work with two-digit numbers in a context they can verify by checking a thermometer outside their own window. Data collection projects become richer as students track daily temperatures, rainfall amounts, and daylight hours across several weeks, then organize their findings into bar graphs and tables that reveal seasonal trends. Reading activities include multi-paragraph passages about topics like why the Earth\\'s tilt causes seasons, how animals in different biomes adapt to seasonal extremes, or how indigenous cultures around the world mark seasonal transitions with festivals and rituals, with comprehension questions demanding inference, main idea identification, and cross-paragraph synthesis. Writing prompts ask students to compose opinion paragraphs about their favorite season with at least three supporting reasons, or to write informational pieces comparing two seasons using data they have gathered. The intersection of personal observation, mathematical analysis, and informational literacy makes second-grade seasons worksheets a powerful tool for building the cross-curricular thinking that distinguishes competent students from truly engaged learners.',\n      objectives: [\n        { skill: 'Add and subtract two-digit temperature values to compare seasonal data', area: 'math' },\n        { skill: 'Read multi-paragraph passages about seasonal science and identify main ideas', area: 'literacy' },\n        { skill: 'Organize and interpret seasonal observation data using bar graphs and tables', area: 'cognitive' },\n      ],\n      developmentalNotes: 'Second graders have developed the capacity to sustain data collection projects over multiple weeks, recording observations consistently and analyzing cumulative results rather than isolated data points. Their growing ability to think abstractly allows them to understand that seasons are caused by Earth\\'s tilt rather than simply accepting that winter is cold, marking a shift from descriptive knowledge to causal reasoning that deepens their engagement with seasonal content.',\n      teachingTips: [\n        'Launch a semester-long seasonal journal project where students record weekly temperature, precipitation, and nature observations, then analyze their data each month to identify trends and make predictions about the coming season.',\n        'Have students research how one specific animal adapts across all four seasons, then present a short oral report with a visual aid, building research skills alongside seasonal science knowledge.',\n      ],\n      faq: [\n        { question: 'How do second-grade seasons worksheets differ from first-grade versions?', answer: 'Second-grade worksheets use larger numbers for temperature and data problems, require reading and interpreting graphs rather than just creating them, include multi-paragraph informational passages with inference questions, and ask students to write organized paragraphs comparing seasons. The cognitive demand is significantly higher, matching the analytical capabilities of seven- and eight-year-olds.' },\n        { question: 'Can seasons worksheets support second-grade research skills?', answer: 'Yes. Seasonal topics like animal migration, weather patterns, and cultural festivals provide accessible research subjects where students can gather information from multiple sources, organize findings, and present conclusions. This scaffolded research process builds the inquiry skills that second-grade standards emphasize.' },\n        { question: 'How do seasonal data projects build math fluency in second grade?', answer: 'Tracking temperatures, counting sunny versus cloudy days, and measuring rainfall over weeks gives students authentic two-digit addition and subtraction practice. Organizing this data into graphs reinforces place value concepts and comparison skills, while interpreting the results develops the analytical reasoning that connects math to real-world understanding.' },\n      ],\n    },\n  },\n\n  // -- FAQ --`,

  'winter': `    },\n    'second-grade': {\n      intro: 'Second graders bring expanding scientific curiosity and stronger mathematical reasoning to winter worksheets, ready to explore the coldest season through data analysis, informational research, and multi-step problem solving that goes well beyond basic counting and identification. Seven- and eight-year-olds can add and subtract within 100, understand place value to 1000, read multi-paragraph informational texts independently, and write organized paragraphs with topic sentences and supporting details. Winter-themed math worksheets at this level present challenges such as the temperature on Monday morning was 28 degrees and by afternoon it had risen 15 degrees, what was the afternoon temperature, requiring students to work confidently with two-digit numbers in realistic weather scenarios. Measurement activities become more precise as students use rulers to measure snowfall depth in inches, compare icicle lengths, and calculate differences between winter temperatures in two different cities. Reading passages extend to multi-paragraph texts exploring how Arctic animals survive extreme cold through blubber, thick fur, and behavioral adaptations, or examining the science behind snowflake crystal formation at the molecular level, with comprehension questions that require identifying main ideas, making inferences, and synthesizing information across paragraphs. Writing prompts challenge students to compose informational paragraphs explaining how a specific animal survives winter, or to write opinion pieces arguing whether winter or summer is better for outdoor activities, supporting their position with at least three reasons. Crossword and word search puzzles feature advanced vocabulary like crystallization, precipitation, and adaptation, pushing spelling and decoding skills to match the expanded scientific understanding that second graders are building. The combination of rigorous academic content with the visceral appeal of snow, ice, and winter wonder makes these worksheets ideal for sustaining deep engagement during the long indoor months.',\n      objectives: [\n        { skill: 'Solve two-digit addition and subtraction problems using winter temperature and measurement contexts', area: 'math' },\n        { skill: 'Read multi-paragraph informational texts about winter science and summarize key ideas', area: 'literacy' },\n        { skill: 'Compare and contrast winter survival strategies of at least three different animals', area: 'cognitive' },\n      ],\n      developmentalNotes: 'Second graders have developed sufficient reading stamina to engage with longer informational passages about winter science topics and to extract key ideas without line-by-line guidance. Their mathematical reasoning now supports working with two-digit numbers in measurement contexts, allowing them to tackle realistic temperature comparisons and snowfall data that would have overwhelmed them a year earlier.',\n      teachingTips: [\n        'Assign a winter animal research project where each student selects an Arctic or winter-active animal, reads about its survival adaptations, and writes a three-paragraph informational report with an introduction, body, and conclusion.',\n        'Create a class weather comparison chart tracking temperatures in your city alongside a city in a different climate zone, having students calculate the daily difference and graph the results over two weeks.',\n      ],\n      faq: [\n        { question: 'How do second-grade winter worksheets build on first-grade content?', answer: 'They move from single-digit to two-digit math problems, from short paragraphs to multi-paragraph reading passages, and from simple recall questions to inference and synthesis tasks. Students analyze temperature data across multiple days rather than single scenarios, and they write organized paragraphs rather than individual sentences.' },\n        { question: 'What measurement skills do second-grade winter worksheets develop?', answer: 'Students measure snowfall and icicle lengths using standard units like inches and centimeters, compare temperatures across days and locations using two-digit subtraction, and record measurement data in tables and graphs. These hands-on measurement activities connect directly to second-grade math standards on measuring with appropriate tools.' },\n        { question: 'Can winter worksheets support second-grade informational writing?', answer: 'Yes. Winter topics like animal adaptations, snowflake science, and weather systems provide rich subject matter for the informational paragraphs that second-grade writing standards require. Students can write explanations of how igloos insulate heat, how animals grow winter coats, or why salt melts ice, practicing topic sentences, supporting details, and concluding statements.' },\n      ],\n    },\n  },\n\n  // -- FAQ --`,

  'weather': `    },\n    'second-grade': {\n      intro: 'Second graders bring genuine scientific thinking and strengthened mathematical skills to weather worksheets, ready to move beyond simple observation toward systematic data collection, pattern analysis, and evidence-based prediction that mirrors real meteorological practice. Seven- and eight-year-olds can add and subtract within 100, read and create bar graphs and pictographs, comprehend multi-paragraph informational texts, and write structured paragraphs with clear topic sentences. Weather-themed math worksheets at this level present challenges such as the morning temperature was 45 degrees and by noon it had risen 22 degrees, but by evening it dropped 13 degrees from the noon reading, what was the evening temperature, requiring students to execute multi-step calculations with two-digit numbers in authentic meteorological contexts. Data projects become genuinely scientific as students record daily temperature, precipitation, cloud type, and wind direction over entire months, then analyze their datasets to identify patterns, calculate averages, and compare their findings to published weather records. Reading passages extend to multi-paragraph explanations of the water cycle in detail, how meteorologists use radar and satellites to forecast weather, or why severe weather events like hurricanes and tornadoes form in specific conditions, with comprehension questions requiring main idea identification, cause-and-effect reasoning, and vocabulary inference from context. Writing activities challenge students to compose weather reports in the style of a real forecaster, write informational paragraphs explaining one stage of the water cycle, or draft opinion pieces about whether their region\\'s weather is better in spring or autumn with supporting evidence from their data collection. The marriage of daily personal relevance with increasingly sophisticated academic demands makes weather one of the most effective themes for developing the scientific literacy and mathematical reasoning that define strong second-grade achievement.',\n      objectives: [\n        { skill: 'Perform multi-step addition and subtraction within 100 using temperature and precipitation data', area: 'math' },\n        { skill: 'Read and interpret multi-paragraph texts about meteorological concepts and weather systems', area: 'literacy' },\n        { skill: 'Design and conduct a month-long weather data collection project with graphed results', area: 'cognitive' },\n      ],\n      developmentalNotes: 'Second graders have developed the executive function skills to maintain a daily data collection routine over several weeks without constant reminding, and the mathematical maturity to calculate simple averages and compare datasets. Their growing ability to distinguish correlation from coincidence allows them to make more sophisticated observations about weather patterns, such as noticing that cloudy mornings often precede afternoon rain.',\n      teachingTips: [\n        'Challenge students to create a weekly weather forecast based on their collected data and observable patterns, then compare their predictions to actual outcomes each Friday, building the scientific habit of hypothesis testing.',\n        'Have students research extreme weather events like hurricanes, tornadoes, or blizzards and present a short informational report explaining how and why the event occurs, connecting worksheet learning to real-world weather phenomena.',\n      ],\n      faq: [\n        { question: 'How do second-grade weather worksheets go beyond first-grade level?', answer: 'They introduce multi-step temperature calculations with two-digit numbers, require reading longer informational passages about meteorological concepts, include data interpretation from graphs rather than just data recording, and ask students to write structured paragraphs explaining weather phenomena. The shift from observation to analysis marks a significant cognitive advancement.' },\n        { question: 'How do weather worksheets develop second-grade graphing skills?', answer: 'Students create bar graphs and pictographs from their own collected weather data, then interpret graphs to answer questions like which week had the most rainfall or how many more sunny days occurred in April than March. This progression from data collection through representation to interpretation covers the full range of second-grade data standards.' },\n        { question: 'Can weather worksheets introduce the concept of climate versus weather in second grade?', answer: 'Yes. After collecting weather data over several months, students can compare their short-term observations to long-term climate averages for their region. This comparison naturally introduces the distinction between daily weather and long-term climate patterns, a foundational concept in earth science that second graders are developmentally ready to grasp.' },\n      ],\n    },\n  },\n\n  // -- FAQ --`,

  'flowers': `    },\n    'second-grade': {\n      intro: 'Second graders bring a deepening understanding of biological processes and stronger analytical skills to flower worksheets, ready to explore plant science through detailed measurement, comparative study, and informational writing that goes well beyond naming parts and sequencing stages. Seven- and eight-year-olds can add and subtract within 100, measure objects using standard units, read multi-paragraph texts independently, and write organized paragraphs with supporting details. Flower-themed math worksheets at this level present challenges such as a sunflower grew 14 centimeters in the first week and 23 centimeters in the second week, how tall is it now if it started at 5 centimeters, requiring multi-step calculations grounded in authentic growth data. Measurement activities become genuinely scientific as students track real plant growth over weeks using rulers, record their data in tables, and create line graphs showing growth trajectories that they analyze for patterns and anomalies. Reading passages extend to multi-paragraph texts exploring how flowers have evolved different shapes and colors to attract specific pollinators, why some flowers bloom at night while others open only in sunshine, or how seed dispersal strategies like wind, water, and animal transport ensure species survival, with comprehension questions demanding inference, main idea synthesis, and scientific reasoning. Writing prompts challenge students to compose informational paragraphs explaining pollination as a process with sequential steps, to compare two flowers using specific measurable attributes, or to write opinion pieces about which flower they believe is most important to its ecosystem and why. The rich intersection of mathematics, biology, and literacy that flower worksheets provide makes them exceptionally effective for the cross-curricular integration that characterizes strong second-grade instruction.',\n      objectives: [\n        { skill: 'Measure and record plant growth data using centimeters, then graph results over time', area: 'math' },\n        { skill: 'Read multi-paragraph passages about pollination and seed dispersal and identify cause-and-effect relationships', area: 'literacy' },\n        { skill: 'Design and conduct a simple plant growth experiment with controlled variables', area: 'cognitive' },\n      ],\n      developmentalNotes: 'Second graders have developed the patience and precision to measure plant growth accurately with rulers and to record data consistently over several weeks. Their growing capacity for causal reasoning allows them to move beyond describing the flower life cycle to explaining why each stage occurs, understanding that pollination leads to fertilization which leads to seed production because of specific biological mechanisms.',\n      teachingTips: [\n        'Set up a comparative plant growth experiment where student groups grow the same flower species under different conditions such as varying sunlight or water amounts, then graph and compare their results to practice controlled experimentation.',\n        'Have each student adopt a flowering plant and maintain a detailed observation journal with weekly measurements, drawings, and written descriptions, culminating in a presentation about their plant\\'s growth journey.',\n      ],\n      faq: [\n        { question: 'How do second-grade flower worksheets advance beyond first-grade content?', answer: 'They shift from identifying flower parts to understanding why those parts exist and how they function together in pollination and reproduction. Math problems use two-digit numbers in measurement contexts, reading passages are longer and require inference, and writing tasks demand organized paragraphs rather than single sentences. The overall emphasis moves from description to explanation.' },\n        { question: 'What measurement skills do flower worksheets develop in second grade?', answer: 'Students measure real plants using centimeters and inches, record growth data in structured tables, create line graphs showing change over time, and calculate growth differences using subtraction. These authentic measurement activities connect directly to second-grade math standards while building genuine scientific inquiry skills.' },\n        { question: 'How do flower worksheets support second-grade science experiments?', answer: 'They provide the vocabulary, background knowledge, and data recording frameworks that students need to conduct simple experiments like testing whether plants grow faster in sunlight or shade. Worksheets scaffold the process of forming hypotheses, collecting data, graphing results, and drawing conclusions, making flower science accessible and structured for seven- and eight-year-olds.' },\n      ],\n    },\n  },\n\n  // -- FAQ --`,

  'nature': `    },\n    'second-grade': {\n      intro: 'Second graders bring emerging systems thinking and a growing capacity for independent research to nature worksheets, ready to explore ecosystems, food webs, and environmental science through data collection, comparative analysis, and evidence-based reasoning that mirrors authentic scientific practice. Seven- and eight-year-olds can add and subtract within 100, read and create data displays like bar graphs and tally charts, comprehend multi-paragraph informational texts, and write organized paragraphs with topic sentences and supporting details. Nature-themed math worksheets at this level present challenges such as a forest survey counted 34 birds, 27 squirrels, and 19 rabbits, how many animals were counted in total and how many more birds were there than rabbits, requiring multi-step operations with two-digit numbers in genuine ecological contexts. Data collection projects become ambitious as students conduct schoolyard biodiversity surveys, counting and categorizing every organism they find in a defined area, then organizing their results into graphs that they analyze for patterns in species distribution. Reading passages extend to multi-paragraph texts exploring complex ecological relationships like how keystone species support entire ecosystems, why wetlands filter water and prevent flooding, or how invasive species disrupt food webs when they enter new habitats, with comprehension questions demanding main idea identification, cause-and-effect reasoning, and evaluation of evidence. Writing prompts challenge students to compose informational paragraphs explaining how energy flows through a food web, to write opinion pieces arguing for the protection of a specific habitat with supporting reasons, or to create field guide entries describing an organism\\'s habitat, diet, and role in its ecosystem. The breadth and depth of nature as a theme ensures that worksheets remain intellectually stimulating across the entire school year while building the environmental literacy that prepares students to be informed and caring citizens.',\n      objectives: [\n        { skill: 'Solve multi-step addition and subtraction problems within 100 using wildlife survey data', area: 'math' },\n        { skill: 'Read multi-paragraph passages about ecosystems and evaluate cause-and-effect relationships', area: 'literacy' },\n        { skill: 'Conduct a structured biodiversity survey and present findings using graphs and written analysis', area: 'cognitive' },\n      ],\n      developmentalNotes: 'Second graders have developed the cognitive capacity to think about ecosystems as interconnected systems rather than collections of individual organisms. They can trace energy through a food web with multiple branches, understand that removing one species affects many others, and begin to grasp that human actions have consequences for natural systems. This systems-level thinking represents a significant leap from the single-link food chains of first grade.',\n      teachingTips: [\n        'Organize a schoolyard biodiversity survey where student teams count and categorize organisms in assigned areas, then combine their data into a class-wide analysis comparing biodiversity across different habitats on school grounds.',\n        'Have students create detailed food web diagrams for a local ecosystem, researching at least six organisms and their feeding relationships, then write a paragraph explaining what would happen if one species disappeared.',\n      ],\n      faq: [\n        { question: 'How do second-grade nature worksheets build on first-grade ecological concepts?', answer: 'They advance from simple three-link food chains to branching food webs with multiple connections, from identifying habitats to analyzing why specific organisms live in specific places, and from counting species to conducting structured biodiversity surveys. Math problems use two-digit numbers, and reading passages require synthesis across multiple paragraphs.' },\n        { question: 'Can nature worksheets develop second-grade research and presentation skills?', answer: 'Yes. Ecosystem topics provide accessible subjects for guided research projects where students gather information from books and approved websites, organize their findings, and present conclusions through written reports, posters, or oral presentations. This scaffolded inquiry process builds the research skills that second-grade standards emphasize.' },\n        { question: 'How do nature worksheets integrate environmental stewardship at the second-grade level?', answer: 'Once students understand how ecosystems function through food web and biodiversity activities, they naturally engage with questions about human impact and conservation. Opinion writing prompts asking students to argue for protecting a habitat, combined with informational reading about real conservation efforts, develop both academic skills and the ethical reasoning that supports environmental responsibility.' },\n      ],\n    },\n  },\n\n  // -- FAQ --`,

  'holidays': `    },\n    'second-grade': {\n      intro: 'Second graders bring expanding global awareness and stronger analytical skills to holiday worksheets, ready to explore celebrations from around the world through comparative research, calendar mathematics, and structured writing that connects cultural traditions to broader themes of community, identity, and shared humanity. Seven- and eight-year-olds can add and subtract within 100, understand place value concepts, read multi-paragraph texts independently, and write organized paragraphs with clear topic sentences and supporting details. Holiday-themed math worksheets at this level present challenges such as a community festival needs 48 red lanterns and 35 gold lanterns, how many lanterns are needed in total, and if 26 have already been hung, how many are still waiting, requiring multi-step operations with two-digit numbers in culturally rich contexts. Calendar math becomes more sophisticated as students calculate the number of days between two holidays, determine which day of the week a celebration will fall on, and compare how many holidays occur in each season using data from a class-created world holiday calendar. Reading passages extend to multi-paragraph texts exploring how the same holiday is celebrated differently across countries, why communities create traditions to mark important moments, or how holidays have changed over centuries as cultures evolve and interact, with comprehension questions requiring comparison, inference, and evaluation of cultural perspectives. Writing prompts challenge students to compose informational paragraphs about a holiday from another culture they have researched, to write comparison pieces examining similarities and differences between two celebrations, or to draft opinion paragraphs explaining which holiday tradition they find most meaningful and why. The rich intersection of mathematics, cultural literacy, and persuasive reasoning makes holiday worksheets a powerful vehicle for building the global competence that modern education increasingly demands.',\n      objectives: [\n        { skill: 'Solve multi-step addition and subtraction problems within 100 using holiday planning scenarios', area: 'math' },\n        { skill: 'Read and compare multi-paragraph texts about holiday traditions from different cultures', area: 'literacy' },\n        { skill: 'Research and present information about a celebration from an unfamiliar culture', area: 'cognitive' },\n      ],\n      developmentalNotes: 'Second graders have developed sufficient cultural awareness to move beyond simply learning that other holidays exist toward genuinely comparing and analyzing why different communities celebrate differently. Their growing ability to see the world from perspectives other than their own makes this the ideal age for holiday worksheets that build empathy and cross-cultural understanding alongside academic skills.',\n      teachingTips: [\n        'Create a class world holiday map where students research one celebration from each continent and pin illustrated summaries to the appropriate location, building geographic knowledge alongside cultural awareness.',\n        'Assign a holiday comparison project where each student researches two celebrations from different cultures that share a common theme like harvest or light, then writes a structured paragraph explaining their similarities and differences.',\n      ],\n      faq: [\n        { question: 'How do second-grade holiday worksheets advance beyond first-grade content?', answer: 'They shift from describing individual holidays to comparing celebrations across cultures, from single-step to multi-step math problems with two-digit numbers, and from reading short paragraphs to analyzing multi-paragraph texts about cultural traditions. Writing tasks require structured paragraphs with topic sentences rather than simple sentences, and research components introduce independent inquiry skills.' },\n        { question: 'How do holiday worksheets build global awareness in second grade?', answer: 'By featuring celebrations from diverse cultures around the world and asking students to research, compare, and present their findings, holiday worksheets develop the cross-cultural competence that helps children understand and respect human diversity. Comparison activities reveal that communities everywhere share common values like gratitude, togetherness, and remembrance, even when their traditions look different.' },\n        { question: 'What calendar math skills do second-grade holiday worksheets develop?', answer: 'Students calculate the number of days between holidays, determine what day of the week a future celebration will fall on, count how many holidays occur in each month or season, and use calendar data to solve elapsed-time problems. These skills connect directly to second-grade math standards on time and measurement while making calendar concepts personally relevant.' },\n      ],\n    },\n  },\n\n  // -- FAQ --`,

  'easter': `    },\n    'second-grade': {\n      intro: 'Second graders bring stronger mathematical reasoning and deeper scientific understanding to Easter worksheets, ready to tackle multi-step problems, detailed life cycle studies, and pattern analysis that transforms the familiar spring celebration into a platform for genuinely challenging academic work. Seven- and eight-year-olds can add and subtract within 100, understand basic multiplication as repeated addition, read multi-paragraph texts independently, and write organized paragraphs with supporting details. Easter-themed math worksheets at this level present challenges such as there are 6 baskets and each basket holds 8 eggs, how many eggs are there altogether, and if 15 eggs are given away, how many remain, introducing multiplication concepts and multi-step reasoning through beloved spring scenarios. Symmetry and pattern activities become more complex as students analyze the geometric properties of decorated eggs, identifying lines of symmetry, creating reflective designs, and extending three-element patterns across multiple repetitions. Reading passages extend to multi-paragraph texts exploring how different cultures celebrate spring renewal, the detailed biology of how a chick develops inside an egg over twenty-one days, or how various spring animals like frogs, butterflies, and birds undergo metamorphosis, with comprehension questions requiring sequencing, inference, and comparison across multiple organisms. Writing prompts challenge students to compose informational paragraphs explaining the stages of chick development with accurate scientific vocabulary, to write procedural texts describing how to plan and execute an egg hunt with detailed steps, or to craft opinion pieces about which spring animal\\'s life cycle they find most fascinating and why. The marriage of festive spring excitement with rigorous second-grade academic expectations makes Easter worksheets a powerful tool for maintaining instructional momentum during a season when outdoor distractions compete for student attention.',\n      objectives: [\n        { skill: 'Solve multi-step word problems involving multiplication as repeated addition using egg and basket scenarios', area: 'math' },\n        { skill: 'Read multi-paragraph passages about spring animal development and sequence biological processes accurately', area: 'literacy' },\n        { skill: 'Analyze symmetry and geometric patterns in decorated egg designs', area: 'cognitive' },\n      ],\n      developmentalNotes: 'Second graders have developed the scientific reasoning to understand biological processes in greater detail, moving from simply knowing that chicks hatch from eggs to understanding the developmental stages that occur inside the egg over three weeks. Their mathematical maturity supports working with multiplication concepts as repeated addition, making egg-and-basket scenarios an ideal context for this foundational skill.',\n      teachingTips: [\n        'Set up an egg symmetry art station where students design decorated eggs using mathematical symmetry principles, first drawing a line of symmetry and then creating matching patterns on both sides, blending geometry with spring creativity.',\n        'Have students research the complete life cycle of one spring animal such as a frog, butterfly, or chick, then create an illustrated timeline with written descriptions of each stage to present to the class.',\n      ],\n      faq: [\n        { question: 'How do second-grade Easter worksheets differ from first-grade versions?', answer: 'They introduce multiplication concepts through egg grouping problems, use two-digit numbers in multi-step calculations, include longer reading passages about spring biology with inference and comparison questions, and require written paragraphs rather than single sentences. Pattern and symmetry activities become geometrically precise rather than purely visual.' },\n        { question: 'How do Easter worksheets introduce multiplication concepts in second grade?', answer: 'Egg-and-basket scenarios naturally model repeated addition and equal groups: six baskets with four eggs each, three nests with five chicks each, or eight children each finding seven eggs. These concrete, visual scenarios make the abstract concept of multiplication tangible and intuitive before students encounter formal multiplication notation.' },\n        { question: 'Can Easter worksheets support second-grade science standards on life cycles?', answer: 'Yes. Multi-paragraph reading passages about chick embryo development, frog metamorphosis, and butterfly transformation provide detailed scientific content that meets second-grade life science standards. Students practice sequencing biological stages, comparing life cycles across species, and using scientific vocabulary like metamorphosis, larva, and incubation in their writing.' },\n      ],\n    },\n  },\n\n  // -- FAQ --`,

  'halloween': `    },\n    'second-grade': {\n      intro: 'Second graders bring bolder creativity and stronger analytical skills to Halloween worksheets, ready to engage with multi-step math puzzles, informational reading about nocturnal biology, and imaginative writing that channels the spooky-fun energy of the season into genuinely rigorous academic work. Seven- and eight-year-olds can add and subtract within 100, work with basic multiplication concepts, read multi-paragraph texts independently, and write organized paragraphs with descriptive details and logical structure. Halloween-themed math worksheets at this level present challenges such as each trick-or-treater visits 12 houses and gets 3 pieces of candy at each house, how many pieces do they collect in total, and if they trade away 15 pieces, how many remain, introducing multiplication as repeated addition alongside multi-step subtraction in scenarios that feel like a spooky adventure. Budget-based activities ask students to plan a Halloween party with a set amount of pretend money, calculating costs for decorations, treats, and supplies while practicing addition, subtraction, and comparison of two-digit numbers. Reading passages extend to multi-paragraph texts exploring the real science behind bats\\' echolocation, how spiders engineer webs with mathematical precision, or why owls can rotate their heads nearly all the way around, with comprehension questions requiring cause-and-effect reasoning, main idea identification, and distinguishing fact from fiction. Creative writing flourishes as students compose descriptive paragraphs about a haunted house using vivid sensory language, write mystery stories with a beginning, middle, and satisfying resolution, or draft persuasive pieces arguing for their ideal costume with supporting reasons. The Halloween theme\\'s unique ability to blend scientific inquiry with creative expression makes it exceptionally effective for developing the balanced literacy and numeracy skills that define strong second-grade achievement.',\n      objectives: [\n        { skill: 'Solve multi-step problems involving two-digit numbers and repeated addition using trick-or-treat and party budget scenarios', area: 'math' },\n        { skill: 'Read multi-paragraph informational texts about nocturnal animals and distinguish scientific facts from fictional elements', area: 'literacy' },\n        { skill: 'Write descriptive and narrative paragraphs using vivid sensory language inspired by Halloween themes', area: 'creative' },\n      ],\n      developmentalNotes: 'Second graders have fully consolidated the distinction between fantasy and reality, which allows them to enjoy spooky creative writing and mystery puzzles as intellectual challenges rather than sources of anxiety. Their growing capacity for sustained creative writing means they can compose multi-paragraph stories with structured plots, and their mathematical reasoning supports working through budget and planning problems that require tracking multiple quantities simultaneously.',\n      teachingTips: [\n        'Assign a Halloween party planning project where students receive a pretend budget and must calculate costs for decorations, snacks, and activities from a priced catalog, practicing addition, subtraction, and comparison while developing real-world planning skills.',\n        'Have students research one nocturnal animal and create a fact-versus-fiction poster that separates real scientific information from common Halloween myths, building critical thinking alongside informational literacy.',\n      ],\n      faq: [\n        { question: 'How do second-grade Halloween worksheets advance beyond first-grade content?', answer: 'They introduce multiplication concepts through candy collection scenarios, use two-digit numbers in multi-step party budget problems, include multi-paragraph reading about real nocturnal animal science, and require organized paragraphs for creative and informational writing. The shift from consuming spooky content to analyzing and creating it marks a significant cognitive advancement.' },\n        { question: 'How do Halloween worksheets develop critical thinking in second grade?', answer: 'Fact-versus-fiction activities about nocturnal animals teach students to evaluate claims and distinguish scientific information from cultural myths. Mystery-style puzzles require logical deduction, and budget planning problems demand strategic decision-making. These analytical skills transfer directly to reading comprehension and mathematical reasoning across all subjects.' },\n        { question: 'Can Halloween worksheets support second-grade narrative writing standards?', answer: 'Yes. The Halloween theme provides uniquely motivating prompts for the narrative writing that second-grade standards require. Students write stories with clear beginnings, middles, and endings, use descriptive sensory language to create atmosphere, and develop characters with distinct traits and motivations, all within the engaging context of friendly spooky adventures.' },\n      ],\n    },\n  },\n\n  // -- FAQ --`,

  'xmas': `    },\n    'second-grade': {\n      intro: 'Second graders bring expanding mathematical confidence and deeper cultural awareness to Christmas worksheets, ready to tackle multi-step gift planning problems, comparative reading about holiday traditions worldwide, and structured writing that channels December\\'s excitement into genuinely rigorous academic work. Seven- and eight-year-olds can add and subtract within 100, work with basic multiplication and equal sharing concepts, read multi-paragraph texts independently, and write organized paragraphs with topic sentences and supporting details. Christmas-themed math worksheets at this level present challenges such as there are 4 family members and each person receives 3 gifts, how many gifts are there in total, and if 5 gifts still need to be wrapped, how many are already wrapped, combining multiplication as repeated addition with multi-step subtraction in heartwarming holiday scenarios. Equal-sharing problems introduce fraction concepts naturally: if a pie is cut into 8 equal slices and there are 4 people, how many slices does each person get. Reading passages extend to multi-paragraph texts exploring how Christmas traditions vary across countries like Mexico\\'s posadas, Germany\\'s Advent markets, and Australia\\'s summer Christmas celebrations, or examining the history of traditions like decorated trees, holiday cards, and gift exchanges, with comprehension questions requiring comparison, inference, and cultural analysis. Writing prompts challenge students to compose informational paragraphs about a Christmas tradition from another country they have researched, to write detailed procedural texts explaining how to make a holiday craft or recipe with sequenced steps, or to craft opinion pieces about what makes a gift truly special beyond its monetary value. The combination of deeply motivating holiday content with two-digit arithmetic, equal sharing concepts, and cross-cultural reading makes Christmas worksheets one of the most effective tools for sustaining academic momentum during December\\'s excitement-filled weeks.',\n      objectives: [\n        { skill: 'Solve multi-step problems using two-digit addition, subtraction, and equal sharing in gift and celebration contexts', area: 'math' },\n        { skill: 'Read and compare multi-paragraph texts about Christmas traditions from different countries', area: 'literacy' },\n        { skill: 'Write sequenced procedural texts and structured opinion paragraphs related to holiday themes', area: 'cognitive' },\n      ],\n      developmentalNotes: 'Second graders have developed the mathematical maturity to work with equal sharing and early fraction concepts that gift-giving and food-sharing scenarios naturally present. Their expanding world awareness makes them genuinely curious about how children in other countries celebrate Christmas, and their growing writing skills allow them to express both factual knowledge and personal opinions in organized paragraph form.',\n      teachingTips: [\n        'Create a holiday gift budget project where each student receives a pretend budget and plans gifts for family members by selecting items from a priced list, calculating totals, making change, and justifying their choices in writing.',\n        'Have students research how Christmas is celebrated in one country they have never learned about before, then present their findings to the class using a poster with illustrations and written facts, building research and presentation skills.',\n      ],\n      faq: [\n        { question: 'How do second-grade Christmas worksheets advance beyond first-grade versions?', answer: 'They introduce equal sharing and early fraction concepts through gift and food division scenarios, use multi-step problems with two-digit numbers, include multi-paragraph reading about Christmas traditions in different countries with comparison questions, and require organized paragraphs for writing tasks. Budget planning activities add real-world mathematical reasoning.' },\n        { question: 'How do Christmas worksheets introduce fraction concepts in second grade?', answer: 'Holiday food sharing provides a natural context for fractions: dividing a pie into equal slices, sharing cookies evenly among friends, or splitting a candy bar in half. These concrete, visual scenarios build intuitive understanding of equal parts and fair sharing before students encounter formal fraction notation, making the abstract concept of fractions tangible and meaningful.' },\n        { question: 'Can Christmas worksheets support cross-cultural learning in second grade?', answer: 'Yes. Multi-paragraph reading passages about how families in Mexico, Germany, Australia, Japan, and other countries celebrate Christmas provide rich material for comparison and cultural analysis. Students discover that while traditions differ in their specific forms, the underlying values of generosity, family togetherness, and community joy are shared worldwide.' },\n      ],\n    },\n  },\n\n  // -- FAQ --`,

  'birthday': `    },\n    'second-grade': {\n      intro: 'Second graders bring stronger mathematical reasoning and growing independence to birthday worksheets, ready to tackle multi-step party planning problems, fraction activities with cake and pizza, and structured writing that transforms the celebration they love most into a platform for rigorous academic practice. Seven- and eight-year-olds can add and subtract within 100, understand early multiplication as repeated addition, work with basic fraction concepts, read multi-paragraph texts independently, and write organized paragraphs with supporting details. Birthday-themed math worksheets at this level present challenges such as you are planning a party for 8 friends and each friend gets 3 party favors, how many favors do you need in total, and if a pack contains 10 favors, how many packs should you buy, combining multiplication concepts with practical reasoning in scenarios that feel immediately real. Fraction activities use cake and pizza as natural fraction models: if a birthday cake is cut into 12 equal slices and 4 guests each eat 2 slices, how many slices are left, requiring students to multiply, subtract, and think about equal parts simultaneously. Reading passages extend to multi-paragraph texts exploring the history of birthday celebrations, how different cultures mark the milestone of turning a year older, or why birthday traditions like candles and singing have persisted for centuries, with comprehension questions requiring inference, main idea identification, and comparison across cultures. Writing prompts challenge students to compose detailed procedural texts explaining how to plan a birthday party step by step, to write persuasive paragraphs arguing for their ideal party theme with supporting reasons, or to create comparison pieces examining how birthdays are celebrated in two different countries. Time-based math becomes prominent as students calculate elapsed time for party activities, determine what time the party will end if it starts at 2:00 and lasts 3 hours, and schedule events in a logical sequence. The deeply personal nature of birthday content ensures maximum engagement while the multi-step problems and structured writing tasks meet the full range of second-grade academic expectations.',\n      objectives: [\n        { skill: 'Solve multi-step party planning problems involving repeated addition, equal sharing, and fractions of a whole', area: 'math' },\n        { skill: 'Read multi-paragraph texts about birthday traditions worldwide and compare cultural practices', area: 'literacy' },\n        { skill: 'Write detailed procedural and persuasive paragraphs related to party planning and celebration preferences', area: 'cognitive' },\n      ],\n      developmentalNotes: 'Second graders have developed the mathematical maturity to work with fractions in concrete contexts like cutting cakes and sharing pizza, making birthday scenarios ideal for introducing equal parts and fair division. Their growing capacity for planning and sequencing allows them to tackle multi-step party organization problems that require tracking quantities, time, and budgets simultaneously, building the executive function skills that support academic success across all subjects.',\n      teachingTips: [\n        'Set up a full party planning simulation where students receive a guest count and budget, then calculate supplies needed, select activities that fit the time available, and write out a complete party schedule with start and end times for each event.',\n        'Use real or paper pizzas and cakes to practice fraction concepts, having students physically divide food models into equal parts for different numbers of guests and record the resulting fractions, connecting hands-on manipulation to symbolic math notation.',\n      ],\n      faq: [\n        { question: 'How do second-grade birthday worksheets advance beyond first-grade content?', answer: 'They introduce fraction concepts through cake and pizza sharing, use multiplication as repeated addition for party supply calculations, include multi-paragraph reading about birthday traditions with comparison and inference questions, and require structured paragraphs for writing tasks. Time calculation and budget planning add layers of real-world mathematical reasoning that first-grade versions do not include.' },\n        { question: 'How do birthday worksheets teach fractions in second grade?', answer: 'Birthday cake and pizza provide the most natural and intuitive models for fractions: cutting a round cake into equal slices, determining what fraction of the pizza each guest receives, or figuring out how much cake is left after a certain number of slices are eaten. These concrete, visual, personally meaningful scenarios build fraction understanding that transfers to more abstract mathematical contexts.' },\n        { question: 'What time and scheduling skills do second-grade birthday worksheets develop?', answer: 'Students calculate elapsed time for party activities, determine start and end times, sequence events in logical order, and create party schedules that account for multiple activities within a set timeframe. These practical time skills connect directly to second-grade measurement standards while giving students genuine agency in planning celebrations they care about.' },\n      ],\n    },\n  },\n\n  // -- FAQ --`,
};

let successCount = 0;
for (const [theme, block] of Object.entries(themes)) {
  const filePath = path.join(BASE, theme, 'en.ts');
  let content = fs.readFileSync(filePath, 'utf8');
  const idx = content.indexOf(MARKER);
  if (idx === -1) {
    console.error('Pattern not found in ' + theme);
    process.exit(1);
  }
  content = content.substring(0, idx) + block + content.substring(idx + MARKER.length);
  fs.writeFileSync(filePath, content, 'utf8');
  console.log('Updated ' + theme);
  successCount++;
}
console.log('\nDone! Updated ' + successCount + ' theme files.');
